# Reverse proxy in front of webhook-receiver.
#
# WEBHOOK_SITE_ADDRESS (compose env):
#   :80                          — HTTP only (local / behind external TLS)
#   hooks.example.com            — automatic HTTPS (Let's Encrypt) on :443
#
# For Let's Encrypt, set a real hostname in WEBHOOK_SITE_ADDRESS and optionally
# add a global { email you@example.com } block below (see Caddy docs).
#
# THIS SITE IS THE PUBLIC SURFACE. It is what Tailscale Funnel (or any reverse
# proxy / ingress) points at, so it must expose the GitHub webhook and the
# health probe and nothing else. The receiver serves the token-gated dashboard,
# the dashboard API, and the simulator from the same upstream.
#
# The separation is by PATH, not by client address, because the client address
# cannot express "local or tailnet but not public" here:
#   * a locally-run tunnel (ngrok, `ngrok http 80`) connects to :80 from
#     127.0.0.1 — identical to a genuine local client;
#   * a tailnet peer arriving through `tailscale serve` is presented to the
#     local listener as 127.0.0.1 as well (tailscaled dials the target itself);
#   * Funnel is the same: the socket peer at Caddy is 127.0.0.1. `X-Forwarded-For`
#     may carry the real client IP on some versions, but Caddy trusts XFF from
#     loopback, so a header-keyed rule is forgeable by any host that can reach
#     :80 directly (it is published on all interfaces today).
# Everything that is not listed below answers 404, matching the receiver's own
# "route does not exist" shape so the public surface enumerates nothing.
#
# The dashboard is reached on the loopback-published receiver port instead
# (compose `127.0.0.1:8081:8080`), optionally tailnet-served with
# `tailscale serve --bg --https=8443 localhost:8081`. See docs/dashboard.md.

{$WEBHOOK_SITE_ADDRESS} {
	handle /webhooks/github {
		reverse_proxy webhook-receiver:8080
	}

	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy webhook-receiver:8080
	}

	# Catch-all: dashboard, /api/*, /simulator, run logs, webhook history.
	# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive, so this only runs when neither
	# path above matched.
	handle {
		respond "Not Found" 404
	}
}
